e":"object","properties":{"assets":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"sourceType":{"type":"string","enum":["acp","lightroom","stock"]},"name":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"mediaType":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"assetType":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"renditionURL":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"size":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}]},"createDate":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"modifyDate":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"score":{"anyOf":[{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"string"}]},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["id","sourceType"],"additionalProperties":{}},"description":"Array of assets. Each has id, sourceType (\"acp\"|\"lightroom\"|\"stock\"), and commonly name, mediaType, assetType, renditionURL, createDate, modifyDate. ACP adds: assetClass, assetType (e.g., \"template\"), collection, path. Lightroom adds: extension, orientation, tags. Stock adds: contentType, pricing (\"free\"|\"core\"), isGenTech, downloadURL."},"metadata":{"type":"object","properties":{"totalHits":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}]},"start":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}]},"limit":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}]}},"additionalProperties":{},"description":"Pagination metadata."},"message":{"description":"Present only when assets is empty. Human-readable explanation for Claude.","type":"string"}},"required":["assets","metadata"],"additionalProperties":{}}},{"name":"adobe.font_styles","title":"Font Styles","description":"Look up every style variant (weights, widths, italics) in a font's family.\n\nArgs:\n    font_identifiers: Fonts whose family styles to look up. Each requires\n        postscript_name; font_id is optional, with the same font_id-first,\n        postscript_name-fallback resolution as the font-details tool (either one\n        resolving is enough; they are not cross-validated against each\n        other). postscript_name must match exactly, including case.\n    locale: Language the returned font family/style names (and\n        recommendation/search group names) are translated into, when a\n        translation is available (default en_US). Hyphen,\n        bare-code, and region-mismatched forms are all\n        normalized to the closest supported locale (e.g. ja-JP\n        and ja both become ja_JP; en_GB becomes en_US); an\n        unsupported value falls back to en_US rather than being\n        rejected. See this\n        server's MCP instructions for the list of supported locales.\n\nReturns:\n    JSON string with a `results` list, one entry per requested font, in the\n    same order as the input. Each entry is either {\"resolved_via\":\n    \"font_id\" | \"postscript_name\", \"requested\": {...}, \"family\": {\"name\",\n    \"slug\", \"foundry\", \"designers\", \"detail_url\", \"language_support\"},\n    \"styles\": [{\"postscript_name\", \"style_name\", \"full_name\", \"weight\",\n    \"style\", \"font_id\", \"preview_url\", \"language_support\",\n    \"library_availability\", \"is_variable_font\", \"axis_values\"}, ...]}\n    or {\"postscript_name\": ..., \"error_code\": \"<code>\", \"user_message\": \"...\",\n    \"requested\": {...}} where error_code is one of invalid_input, not_found,\n    forbidden, upstream_error, upstream_timeout, and user_message is\n    human-readable text safe to show the caller as-is.\n    There is no top-level font_identifier on a success entry — use\n    `requested` (below) to match a result back to its input, or find the\n    input font's own entry inside `styles` by matching postscript_name\n    (its resolved font_id is there too). resolved_via tells you which\n    identifier decided the outcome when both font_id and postscript_name\n    were supplied, since they aren't cross-validated against each other —\n    same meaning as the font-details tool's resolved_via. requested\n    ({postscript_name, font_id — font_id present only if you supplied one})\n    is always present on every entry, echoing exactly what you sent — same\n    field and meaning as the font-details tool's requested. The requested\n    postscript_name may not even appear in `styles` if font_id resolved to\n    an unrelated family.\n    family.language_support is present only when every style in the family\n    shares the same language_support; a style then only carries its own\n    language_support when its languages\n    differ from that shared value. When styles disagree, family has no\n    language_support at all and every style carries its own explicitly —\n    there is no majority-vote default, so never assume a missing\n    per-style language_support means it matches the family's unless\n    family.language_support is actually present.\n    invalid_input / not_found follow the font-details tool's exact\n    semantics (same client-side format check; same \"the font itself\n    doesn't exist\" meaning). forbidden means the font itself resolved\n    fine but its family's style list is restricted for anonymous access —\n    distinct from not_found. upstream_error means the lookup itself\n    failed with a hard error. upstream_timeout means the lookup hit a\n    transient failure — a timeout, a connection error, or a 5xx — so\n    retrying may succeed. weight is the\n    numeric OpenType weight class (e.g. 400, 700) — same field as\n    the font-details tool — and the same class can look visually different\n    across different families. is_variable_font (true/false) is present\n    on every style — the same flag every font-returning tool exposes —\n    so a variable style is never indistinguishable from a static one.\n    axis_values (present only on a variable font's style, and only when\n    that style's named instance carries concrete settings) is that\n    specific named style's own variation-axis settings, e.g. {\"wght\":\n    400, \"opsz\": 12} — the exact values this style uses, not a min/max\n    range (there's no reliable range data available; see the\n    font-details tool's axes field for why). Where the font-level tools\n    can only report axis tags (their `axes` field), this tool reports the\n    actual per-instance values, so it gives axis_values instead of axes.\n\nIf the user wants to see a specific style rather than only read about it,\nand a font-preview tool is available on this surface, pass that style's\nown font_identifier (from `styles`, not the input) to it to show an\ninline specimen — this tool only returns metadata text, never a\nrendered preview.\n","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"font_identifiers":{"items":{"$ref":"#/$defs/FontIdentifier"},"title":"Font Identifiers","type":"array"},"locale":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"default":"en_US","title":"Locale"}},"required":["font_identifiers"],"$defs":{"FontIdentifier":{"description":"A font's identity: mandatory postscript_name, optional font_id. Shared\nacross tools that consume this differently -- font_id is tried first in\nget_font_details/get_font_styles/get_font_preview, used to skip a\nresolution hop in add_fonts_to_cc_selections, and accepted but never\nconsumed in create_font_kit or get_similar_fonts (both look up by\npostscript_name alone) -- see each tool's own docstring for which\napplies.","properties":{"postscript_name":{"title":"Postscript Name","type":"string"},"font_id":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}],"default":null,"title":"Font Id"}},"required":["postscript_name"],"title":"FontIdentifier","type":"object"}},"title":"get_font_stylesArguments"},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"destructiveHint":false,"openWorldHint":false},"_meta":{"resource_name":"adobe.font_styles","_codex_apps":{"resource_uri":"/connector_69312da8e4dc81919370cb86fd172b6c/link_6abb1a24e0d88191bee88b0fb41f697e/font_styles","contains_mcp_source":true},"connector_id":"connector_69312da8e4dc81919370cb86fd172b6c","connector_name":"Adobe","connector_description":"Design, co